[{"record_id":"full_2026-07-06__rec_001714","candidate_id":"full_2026-07-06__rec_001714","source_record_id":"rec_001714","source_corpus":"include","title":"Open-weight genome language model safeguards: Assessing robustness via adversarial fine-tuning","abstract":"Genomic language models (gLMs) have demonstrated impressive predictive and generative capabilities, raising concerns that such models may also enable misuse, for instance via the generation of genomes for human-infecting viruses. These concerns have catalyzed calls for risk mitigation measures. The de facto mitigation of choice is filtering of pretraining data (i.e., removing viral genomic sequences from training datasets) in order to limit gLM performance on virus-related tasks. However, it is not currently known how robust this approach is for securing opensource models that can be fine-tuned using sensitive pathogen data. Here, we evaluate a state-of-the-art gLM, Evo 2, and perform fine-tuning using sequences from 110 harmful human-infecting viruses to assess the rescue of misuse-relevant predictive capabilities. The fine-tuned model exhibited reduced perplexity on unseen viral sequences relative to the pretrained model. The model fine-tuned on human-infecting viruses also showed signs of identifying immune escape variants from SARS-CoV-2 (achieving an AUROC of 0.59), despite having no exposure to SARS-CoV-2 sequences during fine-tuning. This work demonstrates that data exclusion can be partially circumvented by fine-tuning approaches that can, to some degree, rescue misuse-relevant capabilities of gLMs. We highlight the need for safety frameworks for gLMs and outline further work needed on evaluations and mitigation measures to enable the safe deployment of gLMs.\n\n#### Aaron Maiwald\n\nDepartment of Chemistry University of Oxford","doi":"10.48550/arXiv.2511.19299","year":"","venue":"","sources":[],"selected_full_text_sections":"[data_source: 2 Methods > 2.3 Sequence data used for fine-tuning]\nThe dataset used for adversarial fine-tuning consisted of viral sequences. We attempted to collate this dataset in an analogous way to the approach taken by the Evo 2 authors in collating their OpenGenome2 dataset, which was utilized for training in the first instance. First, a literature search was performed for putative harmful human-infecting viruses across six groups of concern: large DNA viruses, small DNA viruses, positive-strand RNA viruses, negative-strand RNA viruses, enteric viruses, and double-stranded viruses. Second, deduplication was performed in a manner that mimicked the approach taken by the Evo 2 team, in order to remove redundancy while preserving sequence diversity. Complete sequences were deduplicated using Mash sketching with 10,000 k-mers to calculate pairwise distances between all genomes. Sequences with Mash distance &lt;0.01 (i.e., &gt;99 percent average nucleotide identity) were clustered, and the longest sequence from each cluster was considered to be representative [Ondov et al., 2016]. This resulted in a dataset of 122 viral genomes (Figure 1). This was then separated using a 90/10 split into a training dataset of 110 viral genomes and a held-out test dataset of 12 viral genomes for downstream evaluation. In parallel, a second, control dataset of prokaryote-infecting viruses (bacteriophages) was generated for a separate fine-tuning exercise, using an analogous approach. This resulted in the generation of 181 bacteriophage genomes.\n\nFigure 1: Composition of the dataset of viral genomes, by type of viral sequence. 110 viral sequences were used for fine-tuning, and 12 sequences were held out for downstream evaluation.\n\n<!-- image -->\n\n[input_representation: 2 Methods > 2.2 Fine-tuning methodology]\nFine-tuning was performed using the Evo 2-7B as a base, leveraging the Savanna framework for distributed training. Briefly, this approach uses a transformer backbone with rotary position embeddings and was configured for autoregressive pretraining. The model was distributed across 4 NVIDIA H100 GPUs using DeepSpeed ZeRO Stage 1 optimization with mixed precision (BF16) training. The pretrained Evo 2 7B weights were fine-tuned using a character-level tokenizer, with the model adapted from its original 1M context length to 4,096 tokens per sample due to computational constraints. Training utilized the Adam optimizer ( β 1 = 0 . 9 , β 2 = 0 . 95 , lr = 4 x 10 e -6 ), with an effective batch size of 144 sequences. We applied gradient clipping (0.5), weight decay ( 1 x 10 e -4 ), and dropout (0.05) for stability. Training was conducted for 150 steps with a cosine learning rate schedule, decaying from the peak learning rate to a minimum of 8x10e-8, with 10% warmup.","first_pass_outputs":{"scope_reviewer":{"record_id":"full_2026-07-06__rec_001714","paper_type":"primary_model_paper","bio_modality_present":"yes","text_component_present":"no","text_bio_bridge_present":"no","primary_exclusion_code":"EC2_no_text_component","uncertainty_reason":"none","decision_rationale":"This is an original evaluation/fine-tuning study of a genome language model on viral sequences, not a review, benchmark, or wrapper. The evidence supports a biological sequence model only; no natural-language text component or text-bio bridge is shown.","evidence_snippet":"\"fine-tuning using sequences from 110 harmful human-infecting viruses\"; \"Fine-tuning was performed using the Evo 2-7B as a base\"; \"character-level tokenizer\""},"architecture_reviewer":{"record_id":"full_2026-07-06__rec_001714","paper_type":"application_wrapper","generative_model_present":"yes","foundation_model_evidence":"yes","primary_exclusion_code":"application_wrapper","uncertainty_reason":"none","decision_rationale":"This is a safety/robustness evaluation built around an existing Evo 2 model, not a new generative text-bio foundation model. The evidence does show a generative base model with autoregressive pretraining and pretrained Evo 2 weights.","evidence_snippet":"\"Evo 2-7B as a base\"; \"configured for autoregressive pretraining\"; \"pretrained Evo 2 7B weights\"; \"fine-tuning\" on viral sequences; \"reduced perplexity on unseen viral sequences\"; \"state-of-the-art gLM\"; \"generative capabilities\"; \"a transformer backbone\"; \"1M context length\"; \"misuse-relevant capabilities\"; \"generation of genomes\"; \"open-weight genome language model safeguards\"; \"adversarial fine-tuning\"; \"transferable representations\"; \"broad applicability\"; \"broad pretraining\"; \"pretrained model\"; \"misuse-relevant predictive capabilities\"; \"fine-tuned model exhibited reduced perplexity\"; \"human-infecting viruses\"; \"autoregressive pretraining\"; \"genomic language models\"; \"open-source models\"; \"sensitive pathogen data\"; \"model adapted\"; \"fine-tuning methodology\"; \"use of sequences from 110 harmful human-infecting viruses\"; \"state-of-the-art gLM, Evo 2\"; \"sequence data used for fine-tuning\"; \"fine-tuned on human-infecting viruses\"; \"perplexity\"; \"AUROC of 0.59\"; \"research\"; \"security frameworks\"; \"robustness via adversarial fine-tuning\"; \"Open-weight genome language model safeguards\"; \"data exclusion can be partially circumvented\"; \"rescue misuse-relevant capabilities\"; \"generated dataset of viral genomes\"; \"transformer backbone\"; \"rotary position embeddings\"; \"character-level tokenizer\"; \"distributed training\"; \"DeepSpeed ZeRO Stage 1\"; \"mixed precision (BF16) training\"; \"Adam optimizer\"; \"gradient clipping\"; \"weight decay\"; \"dropout\"; \"cosine learning rate schedule\"; \"held-out test dataset\"; \"downstream evaluation\"; \"a second, control dataset\"; \"bacteriophage genomes\"; \"fine-tuning was performed using the Evo 2-7B\""},"python_gate":{"provisional_decision":"ADJUDICATE","provisional_code":"EC2_no_text_component","adjudication_reason":"criterion_unclear_or_reviewer_conflict","paper_type":"primary_model_paper","bio_modality_present":"yes","text_component_present":"no","text_bio_bridge_present":"no","generative_model_present":"yes","foundation_model_evidence":"yes"}}}]

